# 0.5 (backlog item 69): a bundle's LASSO is the drawn extent of its
# members, so a non-member drawn inside it is drawn as one of them. The pins
# below put "ts" — a test station wired to the tap, not part of the
# aggregation — inside the lasso of "lag". The source is impeccable; the
# defect exists only in the final coordinates, so the diagnostic comes from
# render(), never from parse(), exactly like the group-band enclosure error
# and the complete-cover error (369) it is modelled on.
#
# It fires here because an AUTHOR COORDINATE is involved: the intruder is
# pinned, and so are the devices whose extent the lasso is. When nothing is
# pinned the engine had the freedom and uses it (959 is the case where it has
# none left), and the message says so instead.
figdown 0.4 topology
title "A pin that puts a bystander inside a bundle's lasso"
node dut "DUT"
node lp "LP"
node lt1 "Tap 1"
node lt2 "Tap 2"
node ts "LT.TS9"
edge dut -- lt1
edge lt1 -- lp
edge dut -- lt2
edge lt2 -- lp
bundle lag "Aggregated link (2 links)" dut--lt1,lt1--lp,dut--lt2,lt2--lp
edge lt2 -- ts
layout
pin dut at=(0,140) width=100 height=70
pin lt1 at=(220,40) width=110 height=40
pin ts at=(220,150) width=110 height=40
pin lt2 at=(220,260) width=110 height=40
pin lp at=(440,140) width=100 height=70
